  { y: '1970', m: 'June', t: 'Relational model', d: '<b>E. F. Codd</b> at IBM publishes “A Relational Model of Data for Large Shared Data Banks”. Data becomes tables of rows.', pos: 'top', key: true },
  { y: '1974', m: 'IBM', t: 'SEQUEL', d: '<b>Chamberlin and Boyce</b> design SEQUEL, later renamed SQL. Relational systems dominate for three decades.', pos: 'bot' },
  { y: '1998', m: 'Name', t: '“NoSQL”', d: '<b>Carlo Strozzi</b> uses the name for a lightweight relational database with no SQL interface.', pos: 'top' },
  { y: '2000', m: 'PODC', t: 'CAP conjecture', d: '<b>Eric Brewer</b> argues a distributed system cannot guarantee consistency, availability and partition tolerance at once. Proved in 2002.', pos: 'bot' },
  { y: '2003', m: 'SOSP', t: 'Google File System', d: '<b>Google</b> publishes its first big infrastructure paper: storage spread over thousands of cheap machines.', pos: 'top', key: true },
  { y: '2004', m: 'OSDI', t: 'MapReduce', d: '<b>Google</b> describes MapReduce: process huge data sets in parallel with just two functions, map and reduce.', pos: 'bot', key: true },
  { y: '2006', m: 'OSDI', t: 'Bigtable · Hadoop', d: '<b>Google</b> publishes Bigtable, a wide-column store. The same year Doug Cutting’s <b>Hadoop</b>, born in the Apache Lucene and Nutch projects, becomes its own project.', pos: 'top', key: true },
  { y: '2007', m: 'SOSP', t: 'Dynamo', d: '<b>Amazon</b> publishes Dynamo: a key-value store that stays writable through failures, using eventual consistency.', pos: 'bot', key: true },
  { y: '2008', m: 'Open source', t: 'Cassandra', d: '<b>Facebook</b> open-sources Cassandra. It mixes Dynamo’s distribution with Bigtable’s data model.', pos: 'top' },
  { y: '2009', m: 'June', t: 'The name sticks', d: '<b>MongoDB</b> and <b>Redis</b> appear and a San Francisco meetup revives “NoSQL” as the movement’s name.', pos: 'bot', key: true },
  { y: '2012', m: 'Cloud', t: 'DynamoDB', d: '<b>Amazon</b> launches DynamoDB as a managed service. NoSQL becomes something you rent.', pos: 'top' },
  { y: '2018', m: 'Convergence', t: 'Lines blur', d: '<b>MongoDB 4.0</b> adds multi-document ACID transactions, while SQL databases learn JSON.', pos: 'bot' },
];
---
<Marq n="02" t="Why NoSQL" dir={-1} />

<!-- THE WALL -->
<section class="chapter wall" id="wall" data-scene="tower" data-chapter="The wall" data-part="2">
  <div class="wall-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">The need for NoSQL</p>
    <h2 class="h-display" data-split>Then the web <em>happened.</em></h2>
    <p class="lede">Distributed relational databases work. At web scale, their guarantees start to cost too much.</p>
    <p class="body-copy">You just saw the price: two-phase commit waits on the slowest site, distributed joins ship megabytes, and every schema change must reach every site. Relational databases were designed to run well on <strong>one machine</strong>. Then the internet brought data that was huge, constant and messy.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="vvv">
    <article class="panel vcard">
      <div class="viz viz-vol" aria-hidden="true">
        <i style="left:6%;height:30%"></i><i style="left:17%;height:45%;animation-delay:.2s"></i><i style="left:28%;height:38%;animation-delay:.4s"></i><i style="left:39%;height:60%;animation-delay:.6s"></i><i style="left:50%;height:52%;animation-delay:.8s"></i><i style="left:61%;height:74%;animation-delay:1s"></i><i style="left:72%;height:66%;animation-delay:1.2s"></i><i style="left:83%;height:92%;animation-delay:1.4s"></i>
      </div>
      <div class="txt"><h3>Volume</h3><p>Big data starts at a few terabytes: enough to span many storage units. Storing it with fault tolerance and backups is hard.</p></div>
    </article>
    <article class="panel vcard">
      <div class="viz viz-vel" aria-hidden="true">
        <i style="top:18%;animation-duration:1.1s"></i><i style="top:32%;animation-duration:.8s;animation-delay:.3s"></i><i style="top:46%;animation-duration:1.4s;animation-delay:.1s"></i><i style="top:60%;animation-duration:.7s;animation-delay:.5s"></i><i style="top:74%;animation-duration:1s;animation-delay:.2s"></i><i style="top:25%;animation-duration:.9s;animation-delay:.6s;background:var(--amber)"></i><i style="top:67%;animation-duration:1.2s;animation-delay:.8s;background:var(--amber)"></i>
      </div>
      <div class="txt"><h3>Velocity</h3><p>Writes never stop and queries must answer quickly. Working on huge data sets means running many processes in parallel.</p></div>
    </article>
    <article class="panel vcard">
      <div class="viz viz-var" aria-hidden="true">
        <i style="left:12%;top:22%;width:34px;height:34px;background:var(--amber)"></i><i style="left:40%;top:30%;width:40px;height:40px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--ice);animation-delay:.4s"></i><i style="left:70%;top:18%;width:0;height:0;border-left:22px solid transparent;border-right:22px solid transparent;border-bottom:38px solid var(--ivory);animation-delay:.8s"></i><i style="left:22%;top:62%;width:70px;height:10px;background:#d8c7ff;animation-delay:.2s"></i><i style="left:60%;top:60%;width:28px;height:28px;border:2px solid var(--ok);animation-delay:1s"></i>
      </div>
      <div class="txt"><h3>Variety</h3><p>Semi-structured and unstructured data with a schema that keeps evolving. Managing that metadata in fixed tables is difficult.</p></div>
    </article>
  </div>

  <div class="sparse">
    <div class="stack-v">
      <p class="eyebrow">Why RDBMS struggle with internet data</p>
      <h3 class="h-sub">Dense and uniform <em>vs</em> massive and sparse</h3>
      <p class="body-copy">An RDBMS assumes data is <strong>dense</strong> and <strong>largely uniform</strong>: every row fills every column. Data from the internet is <strong>massive and sparse</strong>, and often semi-structured. Store sparse data in one wide table and most cells are NULL. Store each record with only the fields it has and the waste disappears.</p>
      <div class="btn-row"><button type="button" class="btn solid" id="sp-toggle">Store it sparse</button><button type="button" class="btn" id="sp-more">+ 4 new attributes</button></div>
      <div class="sp-stats"><div>Cells stored<b id="sp-cells">0</b></div><div>Of them NULL<b id="sp-null">0</b></div><div>Columns in schema<b id="sp-cols">0</b></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="panel sp-panel"><div class="panel-body"><div class="sp-stage" id="sp-stage"></div></div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="emerge">
    <div class="stack-v">
      <p class="eyebrow">Emergence of NoSQL systems</p>
      <h3 class="h-sub">Companies built <em>their own</em></h3>
      <p class="body-copy">An e-mail service needs vast storage but not most SQL features; a social network stores profiles, relationships and posts with images and video. A rigid relational model was too restrictive and offered services these apps did not need, so the companies facing the problem wrote their own systems.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="emerge-list">
      <div><span class="tag col">Wide-column</span><b>Google · Bigtable</b><p>Gmail, Google Maps, web indexing.</p></div>
      <div><span class="tag kv">Key-value</span><b>Amazon · Dynamo</b><p>Inspired DynamoDB, Amazon’s cloud key-value service.</p></div>
      <div><span class="tag col">Wide-column</span><b>Facebook · Cassandra</b><p>Combines key-value and column-based ideas.</p></div>
      <div><span class="tag doc">Document</span><b>MongoDB · CouchDB</b><p>Document stores, open source.</p></div>
      <div><span class="tag graph">Graph</span><b>Neo4j · GraphBase</b><p>Graph databases.</p></div>
      <div><span class="tag">Hybrid</span><b>OrientDB</b><p>Combines several categories in one system.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="storm">
    <p class="eyebrow">How did we get here? The perfect storm</p>
    <div class="storm-row">
      <div><span>01</span><b>Social media</b><p>Facebook and Twitter exploded, with enormous data needs.</p></div>
      <div><span>02</span><b>The cloud</b><p>Services like Amazon S3 (Simple Storage Service) made storage something you rent.</p></div>
      <div><span>03</span><b>Dynamic data</b><p>As developers moved to dynamically typed languages like Python and Ruby, data became dynamically typed too, with frequent schema changes.</p></div>
      <div><span>04</span><b>Open source</b><p>A community that builds and shares tools, plus commodity hardware that keeps getting cheaper.</p></div>
    </div>
    <p class="body-copy storm-note">Not a rebellion against RDBMS. SQL is a rich query language that NoSQL products still cannot match. NoSQL is a <strong>pragmatic response</strong> to the growing scale of databases and the falling price of commodity hardware.</p>
  </div>
</section>
