    <!-- 05 RELATIONAL -->
    <section class="chapter rel" id="table" data-scene="grid" data-chapter="RDBMS recap" data-part="2">
      <div class="rel-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">RDBMS characteristics</p>
        <h2 class="h-display" data-split>For forty years, data lived in <em>tables.</em></h2>
        <p class="body-copy">A relational DBMS stores data in <strong>tables of rows and columns</strong>, and relationships are represented <strong>by the data itself</strong>: a <strong>foreign key</strong> holds the <strong>primary key</strong> of another row, and a <strong>JOIN</strong> follows it. You define structure with a <strong>Data Definition Language</strong> and change data with a <strong>Data Manipulation Language</strong>, both part of SQL.</p>
        <p class="body-copy">Applications say <strong>what</strong> they want, not <strong>how</strong> to get it. The physical layer is abstracted away, so it can change, by adding an index or moving to an in-memory database, without touching the application. Add a mathematical foundation, strong consistency, concurrency control, recovery and decades of tools, and you get a design that still runs most banking and billing. Changes run inside <strong>transactions</strong> with the ACID properties.</p>
        <div class="acid">
          <div><b>A</b><span>Atomicity. A transaction happens completely or not at all.</span></div>
          <div><b>C</b><span>Consistency. Every commit leaves the data valid under its rules.</span></div>
          <div><b>I</b><span>Isolation. Concurrent transactions do not see each other’s half-done work.</span></div>
          <div><b>D</b><span>Durability. Once committed, a change survives a crash.</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="panel rel-tables">
        <div class="panel-head"><span>Sample schema · hover a student</span><span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span></div>
        <div class="panel-body" style="display:grid;gap:16px">
